{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-38096" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -8\\end{aligned}],\\,{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/state.json" "file_sha256" "04b2012d3ccd58ed218c43f42d2370ff52ca4db2ef22e0920f733d8e2e5cc0f2"} {"question" "q-38096" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/assets/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nOur goal is to compute $[T]_{C}.$ First, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot (-1)}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/state.json" "file_sha256" "04b2012d3ccd58ed218c43f42d2370ff52ca4db2ef22e0920f733d8e2e5cc0f2"} {"question" "q-38096" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "acadd934ff31d0ada9b4f4d60544cb65970b845c3163b4f20f304c0f2fd94a85"} {"question" "q-38096" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/state.json" "file_sha256" "04b2012d3ccd58ed218c43f42d2370ff52ca4db2ef22e0920f733d8e2e5cc0f2"} {"question" "q-38096" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -8\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/state.json" "file_sha256" "04b2012d3ccd58ed218c43f42d2370ff52ca4db2ef22e0920f733d8e2e5cc0f2"} {"question" "q-38096" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/state.json" "file_sha256" "04b2012d3ccd58ed218c43f42d2370ff52ca4db2ef22e0920f733d8e2e5cc0f2"} {"question" "q-38809" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/state.json" "file_sha256" "04b2012d3ccd58ed218c43f42d2370ff52ca4db2ef22e0920f733d8e2e5cc0f2"} {"question" "q-38809" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/assets/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 8 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 9 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 5-3\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/state.json" "file_sha256" "04b2012d3ccd58ed218c43f42d2370ff52ca4db2ef22e0920f733d8e2e5cc0f2"} {"question" "q-38809" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "acadd934ff31d0ada9b4f4d60544cb65970b845c3163b4f20f304c0f2fd94a85"} {"question" "q-38809" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/state.json" "file_sha256" "04b2012d3ccd58ed218c43f42d2370ff52ca4db2ef22e0920f733d8e2e5cc0f2"} {"question" "q-38809" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ -22 & -15\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/state.json" "file_sha256" "04b2012d3ccd58ed218c43f42d2370ff52ca4db2ef22e0920f733d8e2e5cc0f2"} {"question" "q-38809" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/state.json" "file_sha256" "04b2012d3ccd58ed218c43f42d2370ff52ca4db2ef22e0920f733d8e2e5cc0f2"} {"question" "q-97116" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/state.json" "file_sha256" "04b2012d3ccd58ed218c43f42d2370ff52ca4db2ef22e0920f733d8e2e5cc0f2"} {"question" "q-97116" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/assets/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nOur goal is to compute $[T]_{C}.$ First, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 5-(-2)\\cdot (-2)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 \\\\ -2 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/state.json" "file_sha256" "04b2012d3ccd58ed218c43f42d2370ff52ca4db2ef22e0920f733d8e2e5cc0f2"} {"question" "q-97116" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "acadd934ff31d0ada9b4f4d60544cb65970b845c3163b4f20f304c0f2fd94a85"} {"question" "q-97116" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/state.json" "file_sha256" "04b2012d3ccd58ed218c43f42d2370ff52ca4db2ef22e0920f733d8e2e5cc0f2"} {"question" "q-97116" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -7\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}10 & -7 \\\\ 24 & -17\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}12 & -5 \\\\ 26 & -11\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 \\\\ -2 & -5\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/state.json" "file_sha256" "04b2012d3ccd58ed218c43f42d2370ff52ca4db2ef22e0920f733d8e2e5cc0f2"} {"question" "q-97116" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 \\\\ -2 & -5\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/state.json" "file_sha256" "04b2012d3ccd58ed218c43f42d2370ff52ca4db2ef22e0920f733d8e2e5cc0f2"} {"question" "q-97120" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/state.json" "file_sha256" "04b2012d3ccd58ed218c43f42d2370ff52ca4db2ef22e0920f733d8e2e5cc0f2"} {"question" "q-97120" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the basis $B$ and standard basis $S$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/assets/dbd020362f950fe8bfb4c592652b9e78a73610fa01a247b0b7ead6f201bf9ea5.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{S}={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1},\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 6}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{S} & ={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/state.json" "file_sha256" "04b2012d3ccd58ed218c43f42d2370ff52ca4db2ef22e0920f733d8e2e5cc0f2"} {"question" "q-97120" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "acadd934ff31d0ada9b4f4d60544cb65970b845c3163b4f20f304c0f2fd94a85"} {"question" "q-97120" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/state.json" "file_sha256" "04b2012d3ccd58ed218c43f42d2370ff52ca4db2ef22e0920f733d8e2e5cc0f2"} {"question" "q-97120" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}11 & 7 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}17 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/state.json" "file_sha256" "04b2012d3ccd58ed218c43f42d2370ff52ca4db2ef22e0920f733d8e2e5cc0f2"} {"question" "q-97120" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/state.json" "file_sha256" "04b2012d3ccd58ed218c43f42d2370ff52ca4db2ef22e0920f733d8e2e5cc0f2"} {"question" "q-140482" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/state.json" "file_sha256" "04b2012d3ccd58ed218c43f42d2370ff52ca4db2ef22e0920f733d8e2e5cc0f2"} {"question" "q-140482" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the basis $B$ and standard basis $S$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/assets/dbd020362f950fe8bfb4c592652b9e78a73610fa01a247b0b7ead6f201bf9ea5.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{S}={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1},\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 5}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{S} & ={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -39 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/state.json" "file_sha256" "04b2012d3ccd58ed218c43f42d2370ff52ca4db2ef22e0920f733d8e2e5cc0f2"} {"question" "q-140482" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "acadd934ff31d0ada9b4f4d60544cb65970b845c3163b4f20f304c0f2fd94a85"} {"question" "q-140482" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/state.json" "file_sha256" "04b2012d3ccd58ed218c43f42d2370ff52ca4db2ef22e0920f733d8e2e5cc0f2"} {"question" "q-140482" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 39 \\\\ 8 & -5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-39 & 8 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -1 \\\\ 9 & 5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -39 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 5 \\\\ 39 & 5\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/state.json" "file_sha256" "04b2012d3ccd58ed218c43f42d2370ff52ca4db2ef22e0920f733d8e2e5cc0f2"} {"question" "q-140482" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -39 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/state.json" "file_sha256" "04b2012d3ccd58ed218c43f42d2370ff52ca4db2ef22e0920f733d8e2e5cc0f2"}] "usage" {"q-38096" []} "basis" 2441 "retractions" [[17592186129569 :question/problem "$[